      <h2>What Does General Liability Insurance Actually Cover?</h2>
      <p>
        A standard ISO Commercial General Liability policy (form CG 00 01) covers three main types of third-party claims:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Third-Party Bodily Injury</h3>
      <p>
        Suppose a customer, delivery driver, or vendor slips on a wet lobby floor or an icy sidewalk outside your office. General liability covers their ER bills, follow-up care, lost wages, and the attorneys hired to defend your company.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Third-Party Property Damage</h3>
      <p>
        If one of your technicians is setting up workstations at a client's office and knocks over an expensive commercial printer, your policy pays to repair or replace the client's equipment.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Personal and Advertising Injury</h3>
      <p>
        General liability also steps in for non-physical claims tied to marketing and reputation, including libel, slander, accidental copyright infringement in an ad campaign, or stepping on a competitor's trademarked slogan.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ What General Liability Does NOT Cover</div>
        <p class="callout-text">
          General liability stops at third-party claims. It will not cover injured employees (that requires <strong>Workers' Compensation</strong>), bad professional advice or software bugs (<strong>Errors & Omissions / Professional Liability</strong>), crashes in company-owned vehicles (<strong>Commercial Auto</strong>), or hacked customer data (<strong>Cyber Liability</strong>).
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Decoding Policy Limits: What Does "$1M / $2M" Mean?</h2>
      <p>
        Most commercial Certificates of Insurance (COIs) list liability limits as <strong>$1,000,000 / $2,000,000</strong>. Those two numbers set separate caps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Occurrence Limit ($1,000,000):</strong> The most your insurer will pay for a single accident or lawsuit during your 12-month policy term.</li>
        <li><strong>General Aggregate Limit ($2,000,000):</strong> The total cap across all claims combined during that same policy year. Hit $2 million in payouts and you are on your own until the policy renews.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Business Owner’s Policy (BOP): The Smart Small Business Bundle</h2>
      <p>
        If your business fits standard small-commercial guidelines (usually under $5 million in annual revenue and fewer than 50 employees), ask for a <strong>Business Owner’s Policy (BOP)</strong> instead of buying standalone general liability.
      </p>
      <p>A BOP rolls three core protections into one contract:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>General Liability:</strong> Third-party bodily injury, property damage, and advertising injury;</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial Property Insurance:</strong> Your building, computers, inventory, tools, and furniture against fire, theft, and storm losses;</li>
        <li><strong>Business Interruption Coverage:</strong> Lost operating income and ongoing payroll if a covered event like a fire forces you to shut down while repairs happen.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>5 Actionable Ways to Reduce Commercial Insurance Premiums</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Put Safety Rules in Writing:</strong> Underwriters often apply loss-control credits when you maintain written slip-and-fall procedures, spill logs, and ladder safety training.</li>
        <li><strong>Collect Certificates of Insurance (COIs) from Subcontractors:</strong> Hire uninsured 1099 subs without proof of their own coverage, and your insurer will fold their invoices into your payroll at year-end audit, sticking you with a surprise bill.</li>
        <li><strong>Pair Workers' Comp Cleanly:</strong> Once you have W-2 staff, keeping general liability and workers' comp with the same carrier avoids finger-pointing between insurers on borderline claims. See our breakdown of <a href="/guides/commercial-workers-comp/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">Workers' Compensation class codes and state rules</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Raise Your Property Deductible:</strong> Moving the property portion of a BOP from a $500 deductible to $2,500 can trim your monthly rate by up to 15%.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask About Association Discounts:</strong> Several commercial carriers knock 5% to 10% off premiums for active members of state trade groups or local chambers of commerce.</li>
      </ol>

