      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Business Liability</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is a Certificate of Insurance (COI) and why do clients demand it? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            A Certificate of Insurance (usually an ACORD 25 form) is a one-page summary from your broker proving your policy is active and meets required limits. Clients and landlords ask for a COI, and often ask to be listed as an "Additional Insured," so your policy pays first if an accident happens on their site.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Do single-member LLCs or sole proprietors really need general liability? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes. An LLC helps separate business and personal assets, but it does not pay your legal defense or stop a court from piercing the corporate veil in a negligence dispute. Even without personal liability, a $500,000 slip-and-fall judgment can wipe out an uninsured LLC overnight.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is an annual commercial insurance premium audit? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            After each 12-month policy term ends, your insurer reviews your tax filings, payroll reports, and 1099 payments to compare actual volume against your original estimate. Brought in more revenue or payroll than projected? You owe the difference. Came in lower? The carrier refunds the overpayment.
          </div>
        </div>
